qa(None, "What is the difference between ridge and Bayesian ridge?",
   f"Same point estimate, different philosophy. Ridge gives one weight vector with lambda chosen by cross-validation. Bayesian ridge puts a Gaussian prior on the weights, uses Bayes' theorem to get a whole Gaussian posterior (mean m, covariance S), and then predicts with a mean and a variance. The posterior mean equals the ridge solution with n*lambda = alpha/beta (prior precision over noise precision). In my data the two are practically the same in accuracy: across {calc('E2 comparisons', nv, '{:.0f}', E2V, 'number of RMSE comparisons Bayes vs ridge')} comparisons the largest absolute RMSE difference was {calc('E2 max abs dRMSE', float(rv.delta.abs().max()), '{:.3f}', E2V, 'max |delta|, metric RMSE')}. What Bayesian ridge adds is an uncertainty statement for each prediction.")
qa(None, "How are the prior and noise variance chosen without cross-validation (evidence maximisation)?",
   f"The evidence is the probability of the training outcomes after averaging over all weight vectors the prior allows. It rewards a good fit but automatically penalises models that need large weights (an Occam factor), so maximising it over alpha and beta balances fit against complexity. The fixed-point updates are gamma = sum beta s_i^2/(alpha + beta s_i^2) (effective number of parameters), alpha = gamma/(m'm), 1/beta = residual sum of squares/(N - gamma). They are computed from the training rows of each fold only and converged in all folds (non-converged: {agg(E2C, '', 'n_nonconverged_folds', 'sum', '{:.0f}', label='E2 non-converged folds')}); I checked my implementation against scikit-learn's BayesianRidge on {agg(E2K, '', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E2 parity configs')} full-sample configurations (largest weight difference {agg(E2K, '', 'max_abs_diff_weights', 'max', '{:.1e}', label='E2 max |dw|')}).")
qa(None, "What are the two parts of the predictive variance?",
   "For a new session with features x the predictive variance is 1/beta + x'Sx. The first part is the irreducible noise of a single observation (how much a woman's score varies around the model). The second part is the uncertainty about the weights, which is larger for unusual feature values and shrinks with more data. Here the first part dominates, so the predictive standard deviation is almost the same for every session; the Bayesian variance is therefore not a very individual uncertainty measure in this dataset.")
qa(None, "How well calibrated was the predictive variance?",
   f"I checked the mean squared standardised error z^2 = ((y - mean)/sd)^2, which should be 1. Within leave-one-woman-out (pooled and longitudinal) it ranged from {agg(E2C, Q_LOPO, 'mean_z2', 'min', '{:.2f}', label='E2 min mean z2 LOPO')} to {agg(E2C, Q_LOPO, 'mean_z2', 'max', '{:.2f}', label='E2 max mean z2 LOPO')}, i.e. roughly calibrated. When trained on the cross-sectional women and tested on the longitudinal women it was between {agg(E2C, Q_X, 'mean_z2', 'min', '{:.2f}', label='E2 min mean z2 cross2long')} and {agg(E2C, Q_X, 'mean_z2', 'max', '{:.2f}', label='E2 max mean z2 cross2long')}: over-confident, because the noise learned on one group underestimates the spread in the other. The predictive variance also hardly varies between sessions, so the model says little about which predictions are more reliable.")
qa(None, "What happens when the evidence says there is no signal?",
   f"The prior precision alpha grows without bound and the posterior mean of the weights goes to zero: the model predicts the training mean with the residual variance. That happened for {agg(E2C, 'median_eff_params < 0.01', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E2 configs with no effective parameters')} of {agg(E2C, '', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E2 configurations')} configurations (for example anxiety from phase and hormones), in agreement with the cross-validated ridge, which picked its strongest shrinkage there. It is the Bayesian way of saying 'these predictors carry no information', and alpha itself is then not identifiable, which is why my comparison with scikit-learn looks at the weights.")
